.\" Manual page for nntp2sql
.TH NNTP2SQL 1 "2026-10-31" "nntp2sql (beta 0.50)" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nntp2sql \- download Usenet (NNTP) article headers into a SQL database
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nntp2sql
.B \-\-host
.I HOST
.B \-\-db\-type
.I TYPE
.B \-\-db\-name
.I NAME
.B \-\-group
.I GROUP
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B nntp2sql
.B \-\-help
.PP
Running
.B nntp2sql
with no arguments prints the full option reference and exits with status 0.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nntp2sql
connects to an NNTP news server, reads the article headers of a newsgroup, and stores them in a
SQLite, MySQL/MariaDB or PostgreSQL database. It supports implicit TLS and STARTTLS, optional
authentication, a pool of parallel server connections, automatic reconnect and retry, and a live
progress bar.
.PP
Each article is stored once per (group, article number). The database carries a unique index on
.IR "(group_name, artnum)" ,
and every write is an update-or-insert, so fetching the same range again updates existing rows
instead of adding duplicates. Only headers are downloaded; article bodies and attached files are
fetched on demand by
.BR isotope-viewer (1).
.PP
Besides downloading, the program can list every group a server carries, remove duplicate rows, export
stored articles to HTML, create empty databases, and save its options to a file and/or into the
database so later runs and the viewers can start from them.
.SH OPTIONS
Options that take a value accept it as the next argument. Operations marked
.I exits
do their work and stop without downloading articles.
.SS News server
.TP
.BI \-\-host " HOST"
News server to connect to (default:
.BR localhost ).
.TP
.BI \-\-port " PORT"
Server port (default 563 with
.BR \-\-ssl ,
otherwise 119).
.TP
.B \-\-ssl
Encrypt the whole connection with TLS from the start.
.TP
.B \-\-starttls
Connect in plain text, then upgrade to TLS with the STARTTLS command.
.TP
.BI \-\-user " USER" "\fR, \fB\-\-pass\fI PASS"
Authentication. Both must be given together.
.SS Database
.TP
.BI \-\-db\-type " TYPE"
Required. One of
.BR sqlite ,
.BR mysql ,
.B mariadb
or
.BR postgres .
.TP
.BI \-\-db\-name " NAME"
Required. The database name; for SQLite, the path of the database file.
.TP
.BI \-\-db\-host " HOST" "\fR, \fB\-\-db\-port\fI PORT"
MySQL/PostgreSQL server (defaults: localhost; 3306 for MySQL, 5432 for PostgreSQL).
.TP
.BI \-\-db\-user " USER" "\fR, \fB\-\-db\-pass\fI PASS"
Database login (defaults: user root for MySQL and postgres for PostgreSQL; empty password).
.SS What to download
.TP
.BI \-\-group " GROUP"
Newsgroup to fetch, for example
.BR alt.binaries.sounds.lossless.jazz .
.TP
.BR \-\-headers\-only ", " \-\-xover
Fetch headers in bulk with XOVER, in batches of up to 500 articles. Much faster. Without it, each
article's full header is requested individually with HEAD.
.TP
.BI \-\-limit " N"
Fetch only the newest
.I N
articles of the group. The default is the whole group.
.TP
.BI \-\-articles " N" "\fR, \fB\-\-count\fI N\fR, \fB\-\-max\-articles\fI N\fR, \fB\-n\fI N"
Like
.BR \-\-limit ,
but keep going until exactly
.I N
articles are stored, even when some article numbers are missing on the server.
.TP
.BI \-\-since " MMDDYYYY"
Start at the first article posted on or after this date, for example
.B 10152026
for 15 October 2026 (month, day, four-digit year; it must be a real calendar date). Downloading
proceeds upward from there. Combined with
.BR \-\-limit ,
.I N
articles are taken going forward from the date. Alias:
.BR \-\-from\-date .
.TP
.BR \-\-resume ", " \-\-continue ", " \-\-from\-last
Continue where the last run stopped: start at the highest article number already stored in the database
for the group. That article is fetched again, which is harmless because writes are upserts, and it
covers a batch that was cut off. If nothing is stored for the group yet the run starts normally; if the
stored numbers lie beyond the server's range (a renumbered group) a warning is printed and the run
starts from the beginning of the range. The article it resumes at is printed, and the run notes when the
group is already up to date. With
.BR \-\-limit ,
.I N
articles are taken going forward from that point; with
.BR \-\-since ,
the run starts at the later of the two.
.TP
.BI \-\-threads " N"
Parallel server connections, 1\-64 (default 16). Stay within your provider's connection limit.
.TP
.BI \-\-retries " N"
Reconnect attempts for a failed request, 0\-10 (default 3).
.TP
.B \-\-upsert
Insert new articles and update existing ones (the default).
.TP
.BR \-\-update\-only ", " \-\-no\-upsert
Only update articles that are already stored; never insert.
.SS Group list and maintenance (these exit)
.TP
.BR \-\-list\-groups ", " \-\-all\-groups
Download the server's complete group list with
.BR "LIST ACTIVE"
(falling back to
.BR LIST ),
store every group in the
.B groups
table inside one transaction, print them, and exit. The stored article count is the estimate
high \- low + 1 until headers are fetched.
.TP
.BR \-\-remove\-duplicates ", " \-\-dedupe
Delete duplicate articles, keeping the oldest row of each set: the same article number in a group, or
the same Message-ID in a group. The same Message-ID in different groups (a cross-post) is kept. The
unique index is then rebuilt. Run this on databases created by older versions.
.TP
.B \-\-export\-html
Write the stored articles of a group to HTML instead of downloading. Needs
.B \-\-export\-html\-out
and either
.B \-\-group
or
.BR \-\-group\-list .
.TP
.BI \-\-export\-html\-out " PATH"
Output file for a single group, or output directory for a group list.
.TP
.BI \-\-group\-list " FILE"
Text file of group names, one per line (lines starting with
.B #
or
.B ;
are comments). Used by
.B \-\-export\-html
and
.BR \-\-skeleton\-db .
.SS Creating a database
.TP
.B \-\-init\-db
Create the tables and record the server settings. A missing MySQL/MariaDB database is created and a
SQLite file is created on first use; a PostgreSQL database must already exist. Continues with the
download when
.B \-\-group
is given, otherwise exits after initializing.
.TP
.B \-\-create\-db
Create the database and tables, then exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-skeleton\-db ", " \-\-create\-skeleton\-db ", " \-\-skeleton
Create the database, tables, settings and group rows, then exit. Without
.B \-\-db\-type
and
.BR \-\-db\-name
it defaults to the SQLite file
.BR nntp2sql.db .
.SS Configuration
.TP
.B \-\-save\-config
Save this run's options to the file
.B ~/.config/nntp2sql/nntp2sql.conf
(created with mode 0600), then exit. No database is touched. The file is read automatically by later
runs and by
.BR isotope-viewer (1).
.TP
.B \-\-save\-config\-db
Save the same options into the database, in a table named after it (database
.B news0
uses the table
.BR news0_settings ;
a SQLite file
.B data/news.db
uses
.BR news_settings ),
then exit. Needs
.B \-\-db\-type
and
.BR \-\-db\-name ;
no config file is written. Give both
.B \-\-save\-config
and
.B \-\-save\-config\-db
to do both. Passwords are stored in plain text in either place.
.TP
.BR \-\-load\-config ", " \-\-read\-config
Read the saved config file (or the one named by
.BR \-\-conf )
and print the settings it contains (passwords shown as
.BR ******** ).
Fails with status 3 if the file is missing. It applies even with
.BR \-\-no\-config .
Without this option the default file is still read silently into every run when it exists.
.TP
.BR \-\-load\-config\-db ", " \-\-read\-config\-db
Read the settings stored in a database and print what they hold and what would change. The database must
be named on the command line with
.B \-\-db\-name
(for example
.BR "\-\-db\-type mysql \-\-db\-name news0" );
a database that only a config file supplies does not count, and the command fails with status 2 without it.
The settings are applied without a prompt unless
.B \-\-confirm\-db\-defaults
is also given. Fails with status 3 if the database has no stored settings. Command-line options still
win.
.PP
By themselves, the two load options only show the settings and exit, without contacting any server or
downloading anything. When the command line also asks for work, such as
.BR \-\-group ,
.BR \-\-limit ,
.BR \-\-since
or
.BR \-\-list\-groups ,
they read the settings and then carry out that work. For example,
.B nntp2sql \-\-db\-name news0 \-\-load\-config\-db \-\-group alt.test
takes the server, login and other settings from the database and downloads the group's headers.
.PP
Combining a read option with a save option copies settings between the two places, then exits:
.B \-\-load\-config\-db \-\-save\-config
copies the database settings into the file, and
.B \-\-load\-config \-\-save\-config\-db
copies the file into the database.
.TP
.BR \-\-confirm\-db\-defaults ", " \-\-ask\-db\-defaults
Before fetching, list the defaults stored in the database that would change this run, showing the
current value beside each (passwords as
.BR ******** ),
and ask
.B [y/N]
before applying them. Without this option stored defaults are applied automatically. It needs a
terminal; when standard input is not interactive the stored defaults are skipped rather than waiting.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-config
Ignore the saved default config file for this run.
.TP
.BI \-\-conf " FILE"
Read options from
.I FILE
(\fBkey=value\fR lines) instead of the default config file.
.TP
.BI \-\-write\-conf " FILE"
Write the current options to
.I FILE
and exit.
.SS Output
.TP
.BI \-\-progress\-width " N"
Width of the progress bar in characters, 5\-200 (default 24).
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Print informational messages as well as warnings and errors.
.TP
.BI \-\-log " FILE"
Also append log messages to
.IR FILE .
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Show the option reference.
.SH CONFIGURATION AND DEFAULTS
Command-line options always win. Below that, the server, port, group, threads and similar settings
stored in the database override the config file, while the NNTP login from the config file wins over
the one stored in the database. Anything still unset takes the built-in default.
.PP
Config file keys:
.BR host ,
.BR port ,
.BR ssl ,
.BR starttls ,
.BR user ,
.BR pass ,
.BR db_type ,
.BR db_name ,
.BR db_host ,
.BR db_port ,
.BR db_user ,
.BR db_pass ,
.BR group ,
.BR headers_only ,
.BR limit ,
.BR progress_width ,
.BR threads ,
.BR retries .
.SH DATABASE SCHEMA
Three tables are created if missing. The options table is named after the database, so several
databases on one server each keep their own.
.TP
.B groups
.IR name " (unique), " article_count ", " first ", " last .
.TP
.B articles
.IR artnum ", " subject ", " author ", " date ", " message_id ", " refs ", " bytes ", " line_count ", " group_name ,
with a unique index on
.IR "(group_name, artnum)" .
.TP
.IB database _settings
.IR key / value
pairs holding the options stored by
.BR \-\-save\-config\-db ,
for example
.B news0_settings
in the database
.BR news0 .
Only letters, digits and underscores are kept in the name. Databases made by older versions kept these in a
single table called
.BR settings ;
the first time such a database is opened its rows are copied into the new table (the old table is left in
place), and the viewers still read the old table when the new one is empty.
.PP
Text is stored as UTF-8. Header bytes that are not valid UTF-8 are converted assuming Windows-1252,
so subjects from older newsreaders (a Latin-1 middle dot, curly quotes) are stored instead of being
rejected by the database.
.SH EXAMPLES
Newest 5000 articles of a group into a local SQLite file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
nntp2sql \-\-host news.example.com \-\-ssl \-\-user me \-\-pass secret \e
    \-\-db\-type sqlite \-\-db\-name news.db \-\-init\-db \e
    \-\-group alt.test \-\-limit 5000 \-\-headers\-only
.fi
.RE
.PP
Everything since 15 October 2026 into MySQL:
.PP
.RS
.nf
nntp2sql \-\-host news.example.com \-\-ssl \-\-user me \-\-pass secret \e
    \-\-db\-type mysql \-\-db\-name news0 \-\-db\-user root \-\-init\-db \e
    \-\-group alt.test \-\-since 10152026 \-\-headers\-only
.fi
.RE
.PP
Save the options once, to the file and to the database, then fetch with only the group named:
.PP
.RS
.nf
nntp2sql \-\-host news.example.com \-\-ssl \-\-user me \-\-pass secret \-\-threads 8 \-\-save\-config
nntp2sql \-\-host news.example.com \-\-ssl \-\-user me \-\-pass secret \-\-threads 8 \e
    \-\-db\-type mysql \-\-db\-name news0 \-\-db\-user root \-\-save\-config\-db
nntp2sql \-\-db\-type mysql \-\-db\-name news0 \-\-db\-user root \e
    \-\-confirm\-db\-defaults \-\-group alt.test \-\-limit 1000
.fi
.RE
.PP
Fetch only what is new since the last run (continue from the last stored article):
.PP
.RS
.nf
nntp2sql \-\-db\-type mysql \-\-db\-name news0 \-\-load\-config\-db \-\-group alt.test \-\-resume \-\-headers\-only
.fi
.RE
.PP
Copy the settings stored in a database into the config file, and the file into another database:
.PP
.RS
.nf
nntp2sql \-\-db\-type mysql \-\-db\-name news0 \-\-db\-user root \-\-load\-config\-db \-\-save\-config
nntp2sql \-\-load\-config \-\-db\-type mysql \-\-db\-name news1 \-\-db\-user root \-\-save\-config\-db
.fi
.RE
.PP
Download and store the server's group list, then browse it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
nntp2sql \-\-host news.example.com \-\-ssl \-\-user me \-\-pass secret \e
    \-\-db\-type sqlite \-\-db\-name news.db \-\-init\-db \-\-list\-groups
hydrogen\-group\-viewer \-\-db\-type sqlite news.db
.fi
.RE
.PP
Remove duplicates from an older database, and export a group to HTML:
.PP
.RS
.nf
nntp2sql \-\-db\-type mysql \-\-db\-name news0 \-\-db\-user root \-\-remove\-duplicates
nntp2sql \-\-db\-type sqlite \-\-db\-name news.db \-\-export\-html \e
    \-\-group alt.test \-\-export\-html\-out alt.test.html
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid or missing arguments.
.TP
.B 3
Bad configuration file.
.TP
.B 10\-15
Network, TLS, NNTP greeting, NNTP command or authentication failure.
.TP
.B 20\-22
Database connection, schema or statement failure.
.TP
.B 30
Runtime error, or a download that finished with failed requests or writes.
.PP
Pressing Ctrl+C stops cleanly: workers finish their current request, everything stored so far is kept,
and the exit status is 130.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ~/.config/nntp2sql/nntp2sql.conf
Options saved by
.BR \-\-save\-config .
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is honoured. Contains passwords; created readable only by you.
.TP
.B <database>_settings
Table inside each database holding the options stored by
.BR \-\-save\-config\-db .
.TP
.B ~/.config/nntp2sql/viewer.ini
Settings remembered by
.BR isotope-viewer (1).
.SH BUGS AND LIMITATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
Without
.BR \-\-limit ,
.BR \-\-articles ,
.B \-\-since
or
.BR \-\-resume ,
a group is read from its oldest article number upward. On large binary groups the oldest articles have
usually expired on the server, so the early part of such a run reports only missing articles. Use
.BR \-\-since ,
.B \-\-limit
or
.B \-\-resume
to begin where articles still exist.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-since
locates its start by binary search on article dates, so it assumes dates rise roughly with article
numbers. A few badly dated posts can shift the start slightly. It needs the server's XOVER command, and
an article that cannot be read during the search is treated as older than the date.
.IP \(bu 2
Some servers number articles sparsely, so a group's stored article count (and
.BR \-\-list\-groups
estimates) can be far larger than the number of articles that really exist.
.IP \(bu 2
Conversion of non-UTF-8 headers assumes Windows-1252. Other legacy encodings can be mis-rendered.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-save\-config
and
.B \-\-save\-config\-db
store the NNTP and database passwords in plain text. Protect the file and the database accordingly.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-init\-db
cannot create a PostgreSQL database; create it first. PostgreSQL is the least exercised backend.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR \-\-write\-conf ,
.B \-\-save\-config
(without
.BR \-\-save\-config\-db )
and
.B \-\-help
exit before connecting to any server or database.
.IP \(bu 2
Only headers are downloaded. Article bodies and files are fetched by
.BR isotope-viewer (1).
